Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.470 --> 00:00:04.230 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  afghans are preparing to hold their  first friday prayers under taliban  control but it's being reported taliban  fighters may be allowed into mosques  with them a confidential report by the  un claims the armed group has priority  lists of people it wants to arrest they  include afghans who've served with the  military and the police as well as  intelligence units the un says the  taliban have been carrying out targeted  door-to-door visits  at kabul international airport the  evacuation of diplomats and afghans  continues the u.s moved 3 000 people on  thursday including those waiting for  visas nato says about 18 000 people have  been evacuated since the taliban took  over on sunday  and there have been several  demonstrations on thursday to mark  afghanistan's independence day at least  two people were killed in the city of  assad about where the taliban used  gunfire to clear crowds but in a rare  move fighters allowed shia muslims to  commemorate the holy day of ashura as  rob mcbride reports from kabul  at kabul airport's main terminal this is  crowd control taliban style  volleys of gunfire scatter people in  panic  at another gate even more desperate  scenes as the airport continues to be  besieged  and at the side of the airport where the  u.s military evacuation is taking place  private defense contractors there use  tear gas  at the british embassy compound long  lines gathered daily to apply for  special visas with promises from the  taliban of amnesty for those who worked  with foreign forces and safe passage to  the airport  but many doubt the taliban's promises  fears raised by its treatment of  protesters wanting to retain the  national flag  deadly clashes with taliban fighters  have taken place in the city of  jalalabad with emotions running even  higher as afghanistan celebrates  independence day  there have been further confrontations  in the city of asadabad  the violence of this independence day  has raised tensions in what is already a  very highly charged atmosphere and by  coincidence it comes on the same day as  ashura the most important date in the  shia calendar a ritual observed here by  the minority hazara community  targeted in the past by isil bombers the  commemoration this year is also  overshadowed by the current upheaval  and concerns the community may face  further alienation under a taliban  government  as i see it if the government is going  to be dominated by just one ethnic group  it makes us worried that the country  could slip back into civil war and  destruction  on the streets of kabul there are signs  of life returning to something like  normal with more business opening and  hopes for a new start but now we are  going everyone was tired of the previous  government people are hopeful for  whatever is coming and that business  will be better but the views of a  nervous population remain mixed  it's better for our future if the  country is free of all these troubles  and his fortunes  we don't want to go back to the previous  taliban era when young people would have  to follow the strict dress code or get  beaten up  with more taliban patrolling the streets  there's also been the return of a  familiar sight traffic police in their  distinctive uniforms back on duty  a small comfort for a population in need  of any kind of reassurance right now  rob mcbride's life for us in kabul rob  the taliban had been saying it was going  to give an amnesty to who people had  worked with foreign forces and in the  military and yet we're hearing about  these door-to-door searches what's going  on  i mean that still remains the assurance  from the taliban but this report issued  through the u.n uh it does make very  worrying reading i think it will gain a  lot of traction here with people in  afghanistan given the taliban's record  in the past and also its reputation so  the report talks about  former military and also intelligence  personnel from the former regime being  targeted a taliban going door-to-door  also worryingly it talks about the  families of those people if they don't  get the individuals themselves the  families are then targeted as a way of  putting pressure on those individuals to  come forward to try to identify uh who  they are and also talks about in for  recruiting informers as try to  presumably set up a network to try to  identify all of these individuals  this as we know goes against what the  taliban has been saying from the very  start it has no interest in  recriminations reprisals this he wants a  different type of regime it wants to uh  start afresh  but we do know that from you know out in  the provinces at local levels as this  conflict uh has swept across the country  there have been cases of uh people being  targeted there have been reprisals  people have been killed and the question  is how much is this the work of taliban  even at a local level or how much is it  just a reprisal between individuals in  villages between families or ethnic  groups i mean it gets very messy and  very confusing at uh as you get out into  the provinces of afghanistan it's it's  not very clear-cut about what is a  targeted reprisal attack so that's the  question really is is uh how much of  this is at a national level that there  might be lists or that might be in kabul  that people are targeted we do know for  example that the defence minister here  about two weeks ago was targeted that's  before the taliban took control they uh  targeted his residents with a suicide uh  bomb attack so that's the question for  people here but many people here are  certainly given the unease about the  taliban taking control certainly will  believe this to be true we'll have  concerns about it and it all adds to the  general pressure to try to get out of  afghanistan you were telling us just a  moment ago rob about the situation in  the at the kabul airport and we were  talking about the taliban checkpoints  the chaos and the streets causing even  more difficulties for people trying to  get there but of course that's not going  to let up is it  now that does still continue we know  that the taliban have closed off the uh  airport road the road to that terminal  uh as a way of trying to limit the  numbers of people heading there of  trying to uh stop uh to try to restrict  the flow of people there so that  continues and we do know that there are  continuing chaotic scenes all around the  perimeter of the uh the airport as the  evacuation flight by the u.s military  continues we've spoken to the civil  aviation authority just about their  plans now we do know that they've been  gradually trying to restore the terminal  building  and they do hope at some point they tell  us to restore commercial flights  remember there hasn't been a commercial  flight a flight that you can buy a  ticket and get on a seat  on a seat of since last sunday so it's  been it's uh the whole of this week has  been without these commercial flights  we've just had the military flights and  the special charters so they hope at  some point but the problem is for the  civil aviation authority they tell us  that the whole terminal building is in  the control of the us military at the  moment and also the operations area  until they say it's okay that uh  civilian staff can move in and they can  start looking at uh restoring commercial  flights then there will be no uh  commercial flights uh but i want to show  you just want to show you something else  that's actually doing the rounds on  social media at the moment and this is  what purports to be an official us  document some sort of permit or visa and  it says that it allows the bearer to you  present this at a security checkpoint  and to consular officers to access  flight evacuation flights to the united  states now words like that in a moment  like this are golden uh it is very  seductive we have no idea if this is  genuine or not it looks genuine but the  fact is that this may have been if it is  genuine it may have been issued to some  people it has now been uploaded onto  social media it has been distributed  people have been printing these out and  we know that a number of people have  been taking to this this to the airport  in the hope of getting on a flight so it  just adds to all of this sense of uh  half truths uh uh rumors etc of whether  something might work and might get you  through a roadblock and get you near the  airport and all adds to the general  sense of chaos here at the moment  especially with regards to the airport  and departures through it rob mcbride  reporting for us from kabul rob thanks  well thousands of families are still  looking for shelter in kabul after being  forced out of their homes by the  taliban's offensive more than 400  families who left provinces in the north  of afghanistan are sheltering in a  school the un had said earlier this  month that more than  244 000 people have been displaced in  just three months  along its partners are providing  temporary accommodation along with basic  health services  ready to use trapatic food for all those  children who are hungry and have made  this journey with hungry stomachs and  unicef is also providing a psychosocial  support to the children who have seen  devastation and impact of war there are  also child friendly spaces here where  children are coming and spending time  facebook twitter and linkedin say  they've changed their services in  afghanistan to protect security and  privacy of users it says afghans can now  shield posts from unknown accounts they  can also remove the ability of other  users to see a list of their online  friends those with facebook and  instagram will receive notifications  about the new features the changes  follow recommendations from activists  journalists and civil society groups  southern haiti's only medical oxygen  plant has been left severely damaged  from sunday's power

